  "standard": "PC.TRIG.B.2",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "prompt": "A rotating platform lifts a robot to height $h = 5\\sin\\theta + 5$ metres as it spins. For $0^\\circ \\le \\theta < 360^\\circ$, at what angle $\\theta$ is the robot HIGHEST? Give the angle in degrees.",
  "answerType": "numeric",
  "primaryAnswer": "90",
  "alternativeAnswers": [],
  "acceptanceMode": "normalized",
  "hint": "The height is biggest when $\\sin\\theta$ is biggest.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "The height is biggest when $\\sin\\theta$ is biggest."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "The largest value sine can ever take is $1$. Which angle in one turn gives $\\sin\\theta = 1$?"
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "$\\sin 90^\\circ = 1$, so the robot is highest at $\\theta = 90^\\circ$ (height $10$ m)."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "Only the $\\sin\\theta$ part changes; the $+5$ is fixed.",
    "Sine tops out at $1$, and on the unit circle the height is $1$ only at the very top: $\\theta = 90^\\circ$.",
    "There $h = 5 \\times 1 + 5 = 10$ m, the highest the platform reaches."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Make a small table",
      "steps": [
        "θ = 0°: h = 5. θ = 45°: h ≈ 8.5. θ = 90°: h = 10. θ = 135°: h ≈ 8.5. θ = 180°: h = 5.",
        "The numbers climb to 10 at 90° and then fall back, so 90° is the peak."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Picture the unit circle",
      "steps": [
        "Sine is the HEIGHT UP of the point on the circle.",
        "The highest point on any circle is straight up from the centre, and that direction is 90°."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "tags": [
    "trig-equation",
    "word-problem"
  ],
